  "sections": {
    "opening": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>You're not weak for how much this hurts. You're not embarrassed that you're still thinking about it. You're not crazy for checking their Instagram at 2 AM even though you told yourself you wouldn't.</p><p>What you're going through is real. And there's a reason it feels like this.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "text"
      ],
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "background-1",
        "container_type": "container",
        "padding_top": 50,
        "padding_bottom": 30,
        "mobile_padding_top": 35,
        "mobile_padding_bottom": 20
      }
    },
    "whats_happening": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Your brain is going through withdrawal. Literally.",
            "heading_size": "h1"
          }
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>When you were in that relationship, your brain was producing dopamine and oxytocin at elevated levels \u2014 the same chemicals associated with reward, bonding, and safety. When the relationship ended, those levels crashed.</p><p>Your brain is responding the way it would to any withdrawal. The intrusive thoughts. The compulsive checking. The physical ache. The inability to concentrate. These are not signs that you loved too much or that something is wrong with you. They are neurological responses to chemical loss.</p><p>That's the science. And the science also tells us exactly how the brain heals from this \u2014 and how to speed up that process.</p><p>This is what the 30-Day Breakup Recovery Journal was built to do.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading",
        "text"
      ],
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "background-2",
        "container_type": "container",
        "padding_top": 40,
        "padding_bottom": 40,
        "mobile_padding_top": 30,
        "mobile_padding_bottom": 30
      }
    },
    "framework": {
      "type": "multicolumn",
      "blocks": {
        "fw_cbt": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Cognitive Behavioural Therapy (CBT)",
            "text": "<p>The most studied approach in psychology for changing thought patterns \u2014 helps you identify the narratives your brain is running about the breakup and about yourself, and systematically replace them with ones grounded in reality rather than pain.</p>",
            "link_label": "",
            "link": ""
          }
        },
        "fw_attachment": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Attachment Theory",
            "text": "<p>Developed by John Bowlby and extended by decades of peer-reviewed research \u2014 helps you understand why this relationship felt the way it did, what your attachment patterns are, and how they have shaped every relationship you have been in.</p>",
            "link_label": "",
            "link": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "fw_cbt",
        "fw_attachment"
      ],
      "settings": {
        "title": "Built on CBT and attachment theory. Not advice.",
        "heading_size": "h1",
        "heading_align": "center",
        "color_scheme": "background-1",
